    "question": {
      ":question/id": "d7a5dd74-6fc3-5cfd-a13a-761d19b3ae98",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following elementary row operations transforms the matrix $K$ into the matrix $L$, where\n\n$$\nK=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,L=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 10 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, notice that the $1$st and the $3$rd rows are the same in both matrices. The only row that has changed is the $2$nd row. \n\nBy considering the given row operations that modify the $2$nd row, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 10 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =L.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}.\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
